Implement a complete FreeRTOS port of dwm3001cdk-lock

Done: assessed feasibility, saved the staged plan, created the sibling application and standalone nRF52833 FreeRTOS port trees, implemented the timing, allocation, mutex, logging, work, semaphore, init-hook, static-thread, serialized OpenThread runtime, static NimBLE/SDC HCI transport, shared MPSL worker, fixed peripheral map, nRF 802.15.4 IRQ/random/temperature contracts, and the MPSL/SoftDevice Controller startup sequencer that claims the frozen interrupt priorities, links only the peripheral GATT/CoC feature set, sizes the static controller pool, registers the entropy source, and publishes the HCI transport contract; ported the pinned NCS opcode dispatcher by compiling it unmodified against a Zephyr-free compat layer and wiring both its command and event halves into the sequencer; brought up the NimBLE host and its FreeRTOS porting layer on a static host task with scheduled controller synchronization and reset recovery, and selected the peripheral-only NimBLE configuration that matches the linked controller features, and implemented the nRF 802.15.4 clock platform on MPSL's source-selecting clock API plus the RTC2 low-power timer with a 64-bit counter extension, compare-race handling, and the timestamper sync channel, and pinned Nordic's Zephyr-free high-precision timer platform for direct compilation; pinned the Qorvo/OpenThread/Nordic/NimBLE source set, and added host, source, and port-purity gates.
Also implemented the persistent key-value store: an append-only log over the two flash pages at 0x7e000 with newest-wins lookup, two-page compaction, and write orders chosen so a power loss loses work rather than data; the format is the port's own and not Zephyr NVS.
Also implemented the Aliro provisioning backend over that store (storage/aliro_prov_kv.c): one key, a usable dev identity on every failure path, oversized records refused rather than parsed, and a factory reset that deletes the key rather than the store so OpenThread's SRP client key survives it.
Also started the BSP: board/time_freertos.c gives uptime from the software-extended FreeRTOS tick and the cycle counter and busy wait from RTC1's free-running counter (24-bit wrap carried in software, waits rounded up plus a guard tick so they are never short, a stopped counter fatal on first use rather than an unbounded spin), and board/fault_freertos.c logs and resets.
Also implemented board/entropy_freertos.c (RNG with bias correction, pooled from the RNG vector so the ordinary request is a copy rather than blocking a radio-priority caller for a hundred microseconds per byte; a caller that outruns the pool polls with only the RNG vector masked) and board/temperature_freertos.c (mpsl_temperature_get, because MPSL owns TEMP and calibrates the LF clock with it); both vendor APIs are asserted in freertos-radio-source-check.sh since nothing local can confirm them.
Also implemented board/log_rtt_freertos.c, a SEGGER RTT up-buffer whose control block is written against the published layout rather than by linking SEGGER's implementation (nothing vendored); RTT and not UART because uart0 is the J-Link OB's VCOM that MCUboot serial recovery rides; no-block-skip mode, a line that does not fit is dropped whole, the write offset never reaches the read offset, and the copy is the only interrupt-masked region, so it must not be called from priority-zero radio handlers. The RTT layout is unverified against real hardware and is a bench check.
Also implemented board/flash_freertos.c, completing the BSP: NVMC with MPSL timeslot arbitration, writes sized to the slot by reading the TIMER0 MPSL resets at slot start, erases in thirty 3 ms partial-erase slices per page rather than one 89.7 ms stall, blocked requests retried rather than failed so no page is left half erased, and a timeslot callback that touches only NVMC and a flag because FreeRTOS API calls are forbidden at interrupt priority zero (the calling task polls instead, deliberately unlike Nordic's Zephyr driver). The NCS workspace is at <repo>/workspace and freertos-radio-source-check passes all 17 rows against it.
Also settled the crypto backend and built it (crypto/): Mbed TLS 3.6.6 standalone with the PSA core on so aliro_prim_psa.c compiles unchanged, and OpenThread left on its upstream default OPENTHREAD_CONFIG_CRYPTO_LIB_MBEDTLS rather than PSA, which removes Zephyr's crypto_psa.c, persistent PSA keys, MBEDTLS_PSA_CRYPTO_STORAGE_C and any ITS backend from the build (OpenThread's SRP client was the only caller wanting a persistent lifetime; all 83 PSA call sites in aliro_prim_psa.c use volatile keys). MBEDTLS_THREADING_ALT over static FreeRTOS mutexes because the OpenThread and Aliro tasks share the PSA key store, allocation on the heap NimBLE already forces, the board RNG as the only seed, and nrf_oberon deferred rather than rejected since its PSA glue is Zephyr-Kconfig nrf_security. freertos-crypto-source-check asserts the contracts nothing local can see and caught PSA_WANT_GENERATE_RANDOM, a Nordic Kconfig option rather than an Mbed TLS one, on its first run.
Also assembled the target build graph, which now links: make freertos-build produces an ELF and a hex and reports their cost, the first image being 11,376 bytes of flash and 6,340 of RAM against 504 KB and 128 KB. It carries the pinned FreeRTOS V10.0.0 kernel and Cortex-M4F port compiled unmodified against a two-header kernel/kernel_compat, the pinned hal_nordic device layer, and the board's startup, tick, timebase, logging, and fault paths; layers are added one at a time and measured, because 128 KB of RAM is the binding constraint and the Zephyr oracle overflows it by 1,752 bytes with the same feature set. board/tick_freertos.c replaces the vendor tick file, which would drive RTC1 through nrf_drv_clock and fight MPSL for the low-frequency clock and which prescales the counter time_freertos.c reads at full rate; RTC1 runs unprescaled at 32768 Hz with the tick on a compare stepped 32 counts at a time, hence configTICK_RATE_HZ 1024 rather than 1000. configMAX_SYSCALL_INTERRUPT_PRIORITY is 4, bounded above by the MPSL low-priority handler that calls FromISR APIs at that priority and below by MPSL at 0 and nRF 802.15.4 at 1, both as static assertions. The linker script walls the key-value store at 0x7e000 off from FLASH so an oversized image is a link error. The first target compile also caught a real defect: board/time_freertos.c used an RTC macro that existed only in the host fake.
Remains: then the otPlatSettings adapter over the key-value store, and the otPlatCrypto surface on the backend above (the millisecond alarm, entropy, reset and assertions are done; the microsecond alarm is off by configuration) now that the pinned radio platform compiles and is started and serviced (the timestamper platform is not needed on nRF52833; its service layer asks for none of those symbols), prove nRF52833 BLE/OpenThread coexistence, then implement and validate the board, UWB, crypto, USB, boot, and DFU backends plus product orchestration.
Gates: preserve the hardware-in-loop Aliro BLE/URSK/Pre-POLL verdict, meet the DW3110 response-arm timing under concurrent BLE and Thread load, fit 512 KB flash and 128 KB RAM, and retain recoverable signed update behavior.
